    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here are three subtle but critical concepts buried in the material, which are especially dangerous traps in quantitative finance:\n\n1. **Uncorrelated does not imply independent:** It is a common mistake to assume two variables are independent just because their covariance is zero. For example, if \\(X\\) is symmetrically distributed around zero and we define \\(Y = X^2\\), their covariance is exactly zero [1], [2]. However, \\(Y\\) is completely dependent on \\(X\\) [2]. \n\n2. **Diverging moments and \"Fat Tails\":** The Central Limit Theorem relies on distributions having a finite variance [3]. However, some distributions have \"fat tails\" that decay very slowly (e.g., proportionally to \\(1/x^2\\)) [4]. For these distributions, the variance\u2014and sometimes even the mean\u2014mathematically does not exist [5]. If you try to estimate historical volatility on data drawn from this kind of distribution, your results will diverge rather than converge as you collect more data [6].\n\n3. **Zero probability does not mean impossible:** In a continuous probability model, the chance of observing any exact real number is exactly zero [7], [8]. However, when you run the experiment, *some* exact number will be drawn [8]. Therefore, an event having a probability of zero means it is extraordinarily unlikely to predict perfectly, not that it cannot happen [8].\n\nWhich of these concepts would you like to explore first?",
